        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Where was Airways International based?\n\nAnswer A: Miami, Florida\n  about: Airways International\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Airways International was an airline based in Miami, Florida.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Anchorage, Alaska\n  about: Airways International\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Airways International was an airline based in Anchorage, Alaska.\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer C: UK, Denmark and Germany\n  about: Airways International\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"The airline section of the business operated with more than 100 aircraft , based in the UK , Denmark and Germany .\" [document 3]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Airways International Airways International was an airline based in Miami, Florida. Airways international was founded in 1979 and began operations around 1984. The airline ceased operations in 1999. The carrier's primary business focus was flights within southern Florida and to the Bahamas. Airways International also operated charter flights. In 1985, Airways began service to the Bahamas with Convair 580 aircraft. Throughout its history, Airways operated a fleet of Shorts 330, Convair 580, Cessna 402, Convair 440, and Piper Aztec aircraft. In 1992, the airline added the Shorts 330 to its fleet and operated. As of 1992, there were 30 aircraft\nDocument 2: Airways International was an airline based in Anchorage, Alaska. Airways International was founded in 1979 and began operations around 1984. The airline ceased operations in 1999. The carrier's primary business focus was flights within southern Florida and to the Bahamas. Airways International also operated charter flights. In 1985, Airways began service to the Bahamas with Convair 580 aircraft. Throughout its history, Airways operated a fleet of Shorts 330, Convair 580, Cessna 402, Convair 440, and Piper Aztec aircraft. In 1992, the airline added the Shorts 330 to its fleet and operated. As of 1992, there were 30 aircraft.\nDocument 3: on its own airlines , along with hotel rooms , from brick-and-mortar stores meant it had been struggling to compete with online rivals and low-budget upstarts . Uncertainty over Brexit and a weak pound were also cited as contributor factors to the operator ’ s struggles . The airline section of the business operated with more than 100 aircraft , based in the UK , Denmark and Germany . Wow Air Wow Air had hoped that a last-minute sale or merger with a higher-profile airline would come through . Getty Images/studioportosabbia In March 2019 , Iceland-based Wow Air , known\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
